Post-Herpetic Neuralgia (PHN)
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Severe chronic pain strictly in a specific dermatome, Duration >3 months post-shingles rash, Severe allodynia (pain from light touch), Resolved active vesicular rash

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-037
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of excruciating, unremitting neuropathic pain causing severe insomnia and psychological distress, requiring urgent potent analgesia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing an acute crisis of agonizing, burning chest pain that is unresponsive to basic analgesics, resulting in an inability to sleep or wear clothing. Urgent physical assessment is required to confirm the neuropathic origin, rule out acute cardiac or pulmonary pathology, and administer immediate targeted pain relief prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The shingles rash healed months ago, but the skin on my ribs burns like acid and even my shirt touching it makes me scream."
History of Present Illness: The patient suffered an acute herpes zoster (shingles) outbreak on the right thorax exactly 4 months ago. The active vesicular rash completely resolved, leaving only mild hypopigmentation. However, the patient reports persistent, severe, and excruciating pain strictly localized to the right T4 dermatome. Over the last 48 hours, the pain has escalated to an intense burning and deep throbbing sensation. The patient describes severe allodynia, presenting as excruciating pain provoked by an entirely non-painful light touch, such as clothing rubbing the skin. The constant, unremitting severe pain has caused severe sleep disruption, profound insomnia, and intense anxiety.
Functional Status: Ambulatory, but highly distressed, pacing the room shirtless to avoid fabric contact.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; acute psychological crisis secondary to unremitting severe pain and exhaustion.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Acutely distressed, exhausted, hyper-vigilant about preventing anything from brushing against the right side of their chest.
Physical Exam: Chest wall inspection reveals complete absence of an active vesicular rash; distinct hypopigmented scarring is noted along the right T4 dermatome. Neurological exam reveals hyperalgesia and exquisite allodynia precisely over this dermatome; the patient flinches violently when the area is lightly stroked with a cotton swab. Cardiovascular and respiratory exams are completely normal. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Severe chronic pain strictly in a specific dermatome, Duration >3 months post-shingles rash, Severe allodynia (pain from light touch), and Resolved active vesicular rash.
Environmental Observations: Patient's shirts are discarded on the floor; environment is safe but reflects severe distress.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking maximum doses of paracetamol and ibuprofen with absolutely zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Post-Herpetic Neuralgia (PHN) - Status: Exacerbated (Pain Crisis)
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pain is strictly superficial, dermatomal, hyper-sensitive to light touch (allodynia), and lacks the deep, crushing central characteristics of cardiac ischaemia.

Herpes Zoster Reactivation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The complete absence of new clustered vesicles on an erythematous base upon careful skin inspection.

Costochondritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pain is neuropathic (burning, allodynic) rather than a deep musculoskeletal ache precisely localized to the sternocostal junctions.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (High clinical distress)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents in an acute pain crisis driven by severe post-herpetic neuralgia. The extreme allodynia and resultant severe insomnia have precipitated an acute psychological and physical breaking point. Standard over-the-counter analgesics are entirely ineffective for this profound neuropathic pain. An urgent on-site clinical assessment was strictly required to rule out acute visceral (cardiac) causes of severe chest pain, physically confirm the absence of a new shingles flare, and immediately prescribe specific, potent neuropathic agents (e.g., Pregabalin) and topical anesthetics. Delaying this intervention would leave the patient in agonizing distress, fulfilling the criteria for urgent after-hours review (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe a neuropathic agent immediately (e.g., Amitriptyline 10mg at night or Pregabalin 75mg) to calm the hyperactive nerve. Prescribe Topical Lidocaine 5% patches to be applied directly over the allodynic area for 12 hours on, 12 hours off. Cease ibuprofen as it is ineffective for this pain phenotype.
Positive Behavioral Change: By carefully applying the cooling Lidocaine patch exactly 30 minutes before bed, the patient will locally numb the hypersensitive skin; the subsequent ability to tolerate pajamas and achieve a block of uninterrupted sleep will strongly reinforce the nightly use of this topical treatment.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days to titrate neuropathic medication dosages.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops deep, crushing chest pain that is internal (not on the skin), severe shortness of breath, new-onset weakness in the limbs, or if new blistering rashes suddenly appear.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is strictly clinical based on anatomical correlation with prior infection.
Clinical Course: Post-herpetic neuralgia is a complex chronic pain state resulting from permanent nerve damage caused by the virus. The goal is significant pain reduction to improve quality of life, rather than immediate absolute cure.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

This specific nerve pain is agonizing but not life-threatening to your heart or lungs. However, if the pain suddenly changes from a burning skin sensation to a deep, heavy pressure inside your chest, seek immediate emergency medical care.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The shingles virus severely damaged the nerve ending under your skin, leaving it permanently "switched on" and sending massive pain signals from even a light touch. Standard painkillers like ibuprofen only treat inflammation, not nerve damage. The new medications work by actively slowing down these misfiring electrical nerve signals.
Actionable Steps: Apply the Lidocaine patch strictly for 12 hours only, then remove it for 12 hours to prevent skin toxicity. Take the Amitriptyline 2 hours before you want to sleep, as it will cause morning drowsiness.

